# Sandtable · Wargame-Driven Development (Behavior Baseline)

This project uses the Sandtable methodology. Any task that builds or changes features, writes requirements, or creates plans must follow the baseline below. At decision points, read `skills/<name>/SKILL.md` on demand before proceeding.

## Four Non-Negotiable Rules (Truthfulness)

1. **Do not guess or fabricate.** Resolve unclear facts by 1) reading code, 2) reading docs, or 3) asking the developer, then write the conclusion back into the PRD / plan / state.
2. **Think before acting.** State assumptions explicitly; surface multiple interpretations instead of silently picking one; call out simpler options when they exist.
3. **Make surgical changes.** Change only what must change; do not "optimize" unrelated code; do not add fallback logic or side quests; every changed line must trace back to the requirement.
4. **Stay goal-driven.** Turn the task into verifiable success criteria and loop until verification passes.

> Violating the letter violates the spirit. "This is too simple to need the process" is the most dangerous rationalization.

## Core Loop (One Battle)

```
INTAKE -> RECON -> OBJECTIVES -> TESTCASES -> PLAN -> MENTAL_REHEARSAL -> REDTEAM -> IMPL_REHEARSAL -> EVALUATE -> INTEGRATE -> VERIFY -> DONE -> FEEDBACK (post-landing loop, re-entrant)
       ^______ any rehearsal finds anomaly / breach / surprise -> verify personally -> ask developer -> fix objective / plan -> rehearse again ______^
```

- **Post-landing loop (FEEDBACK)**: after DONE, user acceptance feedback enters here; every escaped bug = a rehearsal blind spot. Defects go through bugfix root cause (root cause MUST be confirmed by logs at 100%) -> fix -> regression case -> lesson, accumulating the lesson into the global `lessons.md` to feed future RECON / red team / PRD. FEEDBACK is human-in-the-loop; autopilot does not drive it.

- **Three rehearsals as one system**: mental rehearsal asks whether the logic closes; red-team attack asks whether it can be broken; implementation rehearsal asks whether the code can actually be built correctly.
- Rehearsals must run in **isolated subagents**, optionally in parallel, and return `LOGIC_CLOSED` / `HELD` / `DONE` or `ANOMALY_FOUND` / `BREACH_FOUND` / `BLOCKED`.
- **If any rehearsal finds divergence from the plan, anything unexpected, or a successful red-team attack, stop immediately and report it**. The main agent verifies it, proposes a fix or asks the developer, updates the docs, and rehearses again until the whole line holds.

## Slash Commands

`/sandtable-start` first five steps · `/sandtable-autopilot` unattended progression from request to debrief · `/sandtable-recon` reconnaissance · `/sandtable-objectives` goals and red lines · `/sandtable-plan` plan · `/sandtable-refine` iterative revision · `/sandtable-mental` mental rehearsal · `/sandtable-redteam` red-team attack · `/sandtable-live` implementation rehearsal · `/sandtable-debrief` score and choose · `/sandtable-rehearse` rehearsals plus debrief only · `/sandtable-bug` intake acceptance feedback · `/sandtable-bugfix` evidence-driven root-cause fix · `/sandtable-status` status report · `/sandtable-resume` resume from saved state

## State and Memory (Recoverable Across Humans / AIs)

Persist all work under `docs/sandtable/` in the target project: global `project.md` (north star), `constraints.md` (MUST / MUST-NOT red lines), `lessons.md` (global lesson ledger, accumulated across features), and for each feature a folder with `prd.md`, `tests.md`, `plan.md`, `state.md`, `journal.md`, `questions.md`, and `feedback.md` (acceptance feedback ledger). After interruptions, resume from those files with `/sandtable-resume`.

